<h2>The background</h2>
<p>A Uttar Pradesh power distribution company awarded the respondent a 2015 turnkey contract for a 33/11 kV sub-station. Disputes over execution, delay and deductions took the supplier to the MSE Facilitation Council in December 2021 under Section 18 of the MSMED Act. Conciliation failed, the reference proceeded to arbitration through the Delhi International Arbitration Centre, and an award followed in May 2024.</p>
<p>The buyer filed Section 34 objections in August 2024 — but before the Commercial Court at Agra, which dismissed them in January 2026 for want of territorial jurisdiction. The buyer then re-filed in the Delhi High Court in June 2026. A Single Judge dismissed the petition as time-barred, and the buyer appealed under Section 37, also contending that the supplier was not MSMED-registered when the contract was made, so the award was without jurisdiction.</p>

<h2>The limitation arithmetic</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>May 2024.</strong> Award rendered through DIAC under the Section 18 MSMED route; served on the buyer.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>August 2024.</strong> Section 34 objections filed — in the Commercial Court at Agra.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>January 2026.</strong> Agra court dismisses for want of territorial jurisdiction.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>June 2026.</strong> Fresh filing in the Delhi High Court — about five months later.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Outcome.</strong> Agra period excluded under Section 14; the post-January gap unexplained; petition beyond the outer limit.</div>
</div>
<p>The Bench restated the two fixed points of Section 34(3) law: three months plus a condonable thirty days, and no recourse to Section 5 of the Limitation Act beyond that, per <em>Consolidated Engineering</em>, <em>Singh Enterprises</em>, <em>Hongo India</em> and the Chhattisgarh SEB line. At the same time it reaffirmed the challenger-friendly rule as well:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;Section 14 of the Limitation Act is applicable despite the language of Section 34(3) of the Act.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>Section 14 did its work — the entire Agra interlude was excluded. What it could not do was explain the five months between the Agra dismissal and the Delhi filing. With no sufficient cause shown for that gap, the petition fell outside even the extended arithmetic, and on the confined Section 37 standard — the appellate court checks only whether the Section 34 court stayed within its statutory bounds — there was, in the court&rsquo;s words, &ldquo;no factual or legal error calling for interference&rdquo;.</p>

<h2>The jurisdictional point that was never heard</h2>
<p>The buyer&rsquo;s strongest-sounding argument — that the supplier lacked MSMED registration at the time of contract, undermining the Facilitation Council&rsquo;s jurisdiction — died with the limitation finding. A time-barred petition gets no merits hearing. For award-debtors under the MSMED regime, where Section 19&rsquo;s seventy-five per cent pre-deposit already makes challenges expensive, the lesson is that the procedural rails matter as much as the substantive objections.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Fix the forum first.</strong> For MSMED-route awards, identify the correct Section 34 court before filing — seat, territorial jurisdiction and the Commercial Courts Act hierarchy. The wrong choice costs months that Section 14 may not fully give back.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Refile immediately.</strong> Section 14 excludes the wrong-court period, not the time you take afterwards. When a plaint or petition is returned or dismissed for jurisdiction, the clock is running the moment you walk out of that courtroom.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For suppliers.</strong> A limitation defence is a complete answer. Before engaging with the merits of a buyer&rsquo;s challenge, build the service and filing chronology — it may end the case.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The MSMED Act&rsquo;s dispute machinery — reference, failed conciliation, institutional arbitration, award — produces an award like any other for Section 34 purposes, but the challenges it attracts are disproportionately from buyers caught by the statute&rsquo;s pro-supplier tilt. This judgment adds a clean Division Bench statement of the limitation framework for such challenges: Section 14 shelter is real, Section 5 is unavailable, the 120-day boundary is absolute, and jurisdictional merits — however arguable — are forfeited by procedural default.</p>
